{
  "description": "issue #748: de-duplicating the addressed set MUST NOT perturb its insertion order or `who`. `tell [ :b :a :b ]` addresses :b (id 2) then :a (id 1); the repeat of :b is dropped as a duplicate but its FIRST occurrence keeps position 0, so `who` still reports :b (issue #679's rule that `tell [ :b :a ]` makes :b current) and the following `forward 40` applies to :b first, then :a — two moves, in that order, not three and not re-ordered. Every change of the addressed set — here from `tell` — also emits the addressing `primitive` event carrying `addressing: { addressed_turtle_ids, current_turtle_id }`, so a consumer can identify the addressed turtle set (issue #766, spec/rendering.md:193); `ask`/`each` emit one again when the previous set is restored, on every exit path.",
